    {
      "head": "Who is made up?",
      "body": "Nell, her father, mother and the baby are invented, as are the trooper at the licence hunt, Nell borrowing an onion, everything the Byrnes say and do, her class-work headings, the place lines at the head of each entry, and Mrs Hayes’s margin notes, hint notes, spelling corrections and words to Nell. The Byrnes are Irish, like many families near the Eureka lead. Chinese diggers worked the Ballarat goldfield and paid licence fees, but no particular Chinese person here is named or invented."
    },
    {
      "head": "Who is real?",
      "body": "Peter Lalor, Captain John Wellesley Thomas, Raffaello Carboni, Anastasia Hayes and Timothy Hayes were all real. Their actions come from the case-file sources. Lalor’s words on the stump are retold, not quoted; the oath, kneeling and “Amen” come from Carboni’s 1855 book. Later notices say she pushed through the soldiers to reach her chained husband and told his guards what she thought of them, but her exact words are not certain. A later tradition credits Anastasia Hayes with helping to sew the Southern Cross flag. That claim is disputed. She is also said to have nursed the wounded."
    },
    {
      "head": "The school and the sketch",
      "body": "Mrs Hayes taught at the Roman Catholic school Dean Matthew Downing opened on 1 April 1853 in the slab-and-canvas St Alipius chapel, Ballarat East (now St Alipius Parish School, Victoria Street). The image on the diary is Eugene von Guérard’s January 1854 sketch Catholic Chapel on the Gravel Pit Line, Ballarat, State Library of Victoria, accession H12532 (this work is out of copyright; no copyright restrictions apply): https://viewer.slv.vic.gov.au/?entity=IE1446748 The spelling is Alipius. Sources: https://saballarat.catholic.edu.au/st-alipius-history.html and https://eurekapedia.org/Anastasia_Hayes."
    },
    {
      "head": "What really happened?",
      "body": "From the records: the licence hunts; licences burnt on Bakery Hill on 29 November; stone-throwing on 30 November; the oath under the flag; the slab stockade; the notice about lights; Carboni frying steak and noting the empty stockade on Saturday; and the soldiers’ silent march before dawn on 3 December. From her tent, Nell could not have known the soldiers’ numbers or their time of march. She only saw what she could see, in the dark."
    },
    {
      "head": "What isn’t certain?",
      "body": "Witnesses disagree on the time of the attack. Nobody knows exactly how many died: diggers, soldiers, and at least one woman. People still argue about who sewed the flag (women of the goldfields, or tent makers or men using tent cloth) and who fired first."
    },
    {
      "head": "What happened next?",
      "body": "The stockade was taken in minutes. Lalor was badly wounded and hidden by friends. Thirteen men, including Timothy Hayes and Raffaello Carboni, were tried for high treason in 1855 and found not guilty. The licence was soon replaced, and Lalor was later elected to Victoria’s parliament."
    }
